  {
    "requestId": "kb-030",
    "actor": "practitioner",
    "patient": "patient",
    "action": {
      "type": "explain",
      "topics": [
        "procedure",
        "benefits",
        "risks",
        "alternatives",
        "evidence",
        "right-to-stop"
      ],
      "text": "I'd suggest a course of acupuncture: five fine, single-use sterile needles around your right knee and leg, left in for about twenty minutes. It may ease the pain; the evidence is mixed — the UK NICE guideline advises against acupuncture for osteoarthritis, while the American College of Rheumatology guideline conditionally recommends it. The risks are small: soreness, a little bleeding or bruising, feeling faint, and very rarely infection. The alternatives are strengthening exercises and keeping active, physiotherapy, and pain relief through your doctor — exercise matters whatever we decide. You can ask me to stop at any time."
    }
  },
